"""On the Commander — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Four — On the Commander'
HEAD = '''Five passages. The opening sentence fixes what a commander is: “he who sums up the civil and the military in himself is the commander of an army; he who joins hardness to softness is equal to the business of war.” Then comes the most famous judgement in the book: <b>“When men judge a commander they commonly look at his courage; but courage, in a commander, is one part in several.”</b> A brave man will close with the enemy readily, and closing readily without knowing where the advantage lies will not do. There follow the <b>five things a commander must be careful of</b>: <i>li</i>, governing a multitude as one governs a few; <i>bei</i>, going out of the gate as though the enemy were in sight; <i>guo</i>, facing the enemy without a thought of living; <i>jie</i>, being as wary after a victory as at the first engagement; <i>yue</i>, keeping the regulations few and unfussy. When the Qing editors of the imperial library praised the <i>Wuzi</i> for holding opinions “not at all at odds with what is right,” these five words are what they quoted.

The second passage sets out the <b>four hinges</b> — of spirit, of ground, of affairs, of strength. A host of a million, its weight disposed by one man: that is the hinge of spirit. A narrow road and a hard pass, a famous mountain and a great barrier, where ten men holding it stop a thousand: the hinge of ground. Good use of spies, light troops coming and going, the enemy’s numbers dispersed and his ruler and ministers set against one another: the hinge of affairs. Sound axles, handy oars, soldiers practised in formation and horses schooled in the gallop: the hinge of strength. Knowing these four makes a commander possible, but he must still have the authority, the virtue, the benevolence and the courage to lead his men and steady them — “get him and the state is strong; lose him and the state is lost.”

The third passage gives the three kinds of awe: drums and bells for the ear, banners and flags for the eye, prohibitions and punishments for the heart. The fourth is a <b>list of twelve weaknesses in an enemy commander</b> — foolish and trusting, so deceive him; greedy and careless of his name, so buy him; changeable and unplanning, so wear him out; rich above and poor below, so divide them — down to the last, which is not an error but a slackness: “if he stays long in one place and his officers grow idle and his army careless, you may creep up and surprise him.” The last passage is the most practical thing in the book. Two armies face each other and you do not know who commands the other one: how do you find out? Send a man of low rank but brave, with a light picked force, to try him — <b>and order him to lose</b>. If the enemy pursues without overdoing it, and pretends not to see the bait, he is a wise commander: do not fight him. If his men shout and scramble, if they chase as though afraid of not catching up and grab as though afraid of missing, he is a fool: however many he has, he can be taken. <b>A small battle thrown away on purpose, in exchange for the other commander’s character.</b>'''

PARA = {
 'p4-1': 'Wu Qi said: “He who sums up the civil and the military in himself is the commander of an army; he who joins hardness to softness is equal to the business of war. When men judge a commander they commonly look at his courage; but courage, in a commander, is one part in several and no more. A brave man will certainly close readily, and to close readily without knowing where the advantage lies will not do. Therefore the things a commander must be careful of are five: first, governance; second, preparedness; third, resolution; fourth, wariness; fifth, restraint. Governance is to govern a multitude as one governs a few. Preparedness is to go out of the gate as though the enemy were in sight. Resolution is to face the enemy with no thought of living. Wariness is, after a victory, to be as careful as at the first engagement. Restraint is to keep the regulations few and unfussy. To receive the command without demur, and to speak of returning only when the enemy is broken — this is the commander’s proper conduct. And so, on the day the army marches out, there is the glory of dying and no disgrace of living.” (“Sums up the civil and the military” means taking both in hand at once. “No thought of living” is to hold no reservation about surviving. “Restraint” means few articles of regulation. That a military classic should cut courage down to “one part in several” is not common. Of the five, the fourth is most characteristic of Wu Qi: after you have won, go on as though you had not yet fought. Both witnesses transpose two characters in “on the day the army marches out”; the base text construes and is kept, and the Sibu congkan text further miswrites “day” as “say.” The Siku text adds a character after “receive the command without demur.”)',
 'p4-2': 'Wu Qi said: “There are four hinges in the use of troops: first the hinge of spirit, second the hinge of ground, third the hinge of affairs, fourth the hinge of strength. When the host of a whole army, a force of a million, has its weight and lightness disposed by one man — that is the hinge of spirit. When the road is narrow and the way hard, with famous mountains and great barriers, where ten men holding a place cannot be passed by a thousand — that is the hinge of ground. When spies are well used and light troops go to and fro, so that the enemy’s numbers are scattered and his ruler and ministers blame one another, high and low reproach one another — that is the hinge of affairs. When chariot axles are sound and boats have handy oars, when the soldiers are practised in formation and the horses schooled in the gallop — that is the hinge of strength. He who knows these four may be made a commander. Even so, his authority, his virtue, his benevolence and his courage must all be enough to lead those under him and settle the multitude, to frighten the enemy and resolve what is doubtful; and when he gives an order, none below him will dare transgress it, and wherever he is, no raider will dare face him. Get him and the state is strong; lose him and the state is lost. This is what is called a good commander.” (A “hinge,” <i>ji</i>, is the point on which a thing turns. “Axles” are the iron keys at the ends of the axle that hold the wheels on; “oars” are the sweep and the paddle. The four hinges are four places where a man can take hold: the minds of men, the lie of the land, division sown in the enemy, and equipment and training. The hinge of spirit stands first and hangs on one man alone, which is why what follows is the list of things that one man must have. The Siku text reads “none below dare <i>venture</i> to transgress” and adds a connective before “no raider will dare face him.” The base text here writes the word for “schooled” with a variant graph; this page uses the form the book itself uses elsewhere, and the colophon says so.)',
 'p4-3': 'Wu Qi said: “The war drum and the bronze bell are what strike awe into the ear; banners and flags are what strike awe into the eye; prohibitions and punishments are what strike awe into the heart. Since the ear takes its awe from sound, the sound cannot but be clear; since the eye takes its awe from colour, the colour cannot but be bright; since the heart takes its awe from punishment, the punishment cannot but be severe. If these three are not established, then though a man hold his state, he will be beaten by the enemy. Hence the saying: where the commander waves, none fail to move with him; where the commander points, none fail to go forward and die.” (The <i>pi</i> is the small drum carried in an army; the <i>duo</i> is a large hand-bell with a clapper; the flags are those used for signalling. Ear, eye and heart each get an instrument and each a standard: clear, bright, severe. What the passage is really about is the perceptibility of an order — if it cannot be heard plainly, seen plainly and feared truly, it never reaches the men. The base text prints a vertical-form colon here, which this page has changed to the ordinary one.)',
 'p4-4': 'Wu Qi said: “The essential thing in any battle is first to take the measure of the enemy’s commander and examine his abilities, and then, going by the shape he shows, to apply the expedient that fits: so you will spend no effort and the work will be done. If his commander is foolish and trusts people, he can be deceived and lured. If he is greedy and careless of his reputation, he can be bought and bribed. If he changes readily and lays no plans, he can be worn out and put to straits. If those above are rich and arrogant and those below poor and resentful, they can be split apart and worked upon. If he is much given to doubt in advancing and withdrawing and his men have nothing to rely on, he can be shaken and made to run. If his officers think little of their commander and have their minds on going home, block the easy roads and leave the difficult ones open, and he can be waylaid and taken. If his road forward is easy and his road back hard, he can be enticed to come on. If his road forward is hard and his road back easy, he can be pressed close and struck. If he camps on low wet ground where the water has no outlet and heavy rain comes again and again, he can be flooded and drowned. If he camps in a waste marsh, in rank scrub and foul undergrowth, and the wind gets up repeatedly, he can be burnt out and destroyed. If he stays long in one place and does not move, and his officers grow idle and his army careless, he can be crept upon and surprised.” (“Take the measure of the commander” means sizing up the man himself. “Block the easy roads and leave the difficult ones open” is to shut the good road and leave the bad one, so that he must take the bad one. Of the twelve, the first six read the enemy commander’s temper and the last six read where he has put his army. The last of all is not a blunder but a slackness — the army has been in the field a long time and has let itself go, which is the commonest failing and the hardest to notice. The Siku text expands the clause “going by the shape he shows, apply the expedient” with two extra particles.)',
 'p4-5': 'Marquis Wu asked: “The two armies are in sight of each other and I do not know who commands theirs. I wish to take his measure. What is the method?” Wu Qi replied: “Have a man of low rank but brave take a light body of picked troops and try him — and let them seek only to be driven off, not to gain anything. Then watch how the enemy comes on. If every sitting and rising is by rule; if in pursuing the beaten he makes as though he could not catch them, and on seeing an advantage makes as though he did not know of it — a commander of that sort is called a wise commander, and you should not engage him. But if his men shout and jabber, if his banners are in disorder, if his soldiers go and stop as they please, if his weapons are held now upright and now crosswise, if in pursuing the beaten he seems afraid he will not catch them and at the sight of an advantage afraid he will not get it — this is a foolish commander, and however many his men, he can be taken.” (“Take his measure” is to appraise a man as one appraises a horse. “Seek only to be driven off, not to gain anything” is the heart of the test: the man sent out is under orders to lose. A small engagement thrown away on purpose, in exchange for a dossier on the opposing commander. It is the most immediately usable passage in the book, and the most characteristic of Wu Qi: he does not ask how many men the other side has; he asks who is in charge of them. The Siku text ends the sentence with a different final particle; the Sibu congkan text writes “taken” with a rare variant graph.)',
}
